u_	au) ar{
u}_	au$, exploiting the hadronic $	au$ decay to $ho^-
u_	au$ to circumvent the experimental challenge of direct $	au$ reconstruction. The differential decay rate is expressed in terms of measurable kinematic variables $	heta^*$, $E_ho$, $	heta_ho$, $\chi_ho$, and $q^2$ -- with complete coefficient functions provided for scalar, pseudoscalar, vector, axialvector, and tensor new-physics interactions. From this distribution we construct a set of integrated observables, including the $D^*$ polarization fractions and lepton-side forward-backward and azimuthal asymmetries, which isolate distinct combinations of helicity amplitudes. We also show that by decomposing the vector-meson energy spectrum into its longitudinal ($L$) and transverse ($T$) helicity sectors, two additional analyzing powers ($\alpha_L$ and $\alpha_T$) related to the measurement of $	au$ polarization $P_	au^{D^*}(q^2)$ are obtained. Without information on the polarization of meson $V$, the spin analyzing power of $ho$ is much larger than that of $a_1$ with $\alpha_ho=0.45$ and $\alpha_{a_1}=0.02$. We further perform a numerical analysis using current experimental data on $R_{D^*}$ and the $D^*$ longitudinal polarization fraction $\langle F_L^{D^*}angle$ to constrain the complex new-physics couplings $g_L$, $g_R$, $g_P$, and $g_T$, revealing the correlations among them.
